#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183943 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/id #uuid "9ef9f522-9802-5d91-8610-1341722d7920" :question/math-academy-id "e-1429" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2 +7x -13$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 -7x +13)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 -7x+13\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x +{\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{13}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 -{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2} -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  -\\left({\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2 + {\\color{purple}{13}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-  {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac72}}\\right)^2  +\\dfrac{3}4.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+7x-13 & =-({(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{3}{4}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186183944 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "b38247c0-cb0a-5fa0-afed-45c7e1ba9e3c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1430" :question/problem "Rewrite $2x^2 - 6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2\\left(x^2 - 3x + \\dfrac{1}{2} \\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 3x + {\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x +  {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* $\\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple} \\dfrac{1}{2} }$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2-{\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}x + \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2} - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\left({\\color{blue}\\dfrac{3}{2}}\\right)^2+ {\\color{purple}\\dfrac{1}{2}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x - \\dfrac{3}{2} \\right)^2 - \\dfrac{7}{4}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-3x+\\frac{1}{2}) & =2({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =2{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184115 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/id #uuid "10318f8d-0396-53de-af20-e3b9ee6ac3d4" :question/math-academy-id "e-9932" :question/problem "Rewrite $-x^2+6x+1$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-(x^2 - 6x -1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 6x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}{2}}$ as a factor:\n\n$$\nx^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${\\color{blue}{3}}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}{1}}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2 - {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue}{3}}x + {\\color{blue}{3}}^2} - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2 - {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - {\\color{blue}{3}}^2- {\\color{purple}{1}}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-{\\color{blue}{3}})^2 - 10\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+6x+1 & =-((x-3)^{2}-10) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}+10 \\\\  & =10-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184116 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/id #uuid "8b469eb3-6577-588c-aa5b-7d168ea6a0dd" :question/math-academy-id "e-9933" :question/problem "Rewrite $-2x^2+20x - 40$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-2.$ So, we take out a factor of $-2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-2\\left(x^2 - 10x + 20\\right)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nx^2 - 10x  + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with ${\\color{red}2}$ as a factor: \n\n$$\nx^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${\\color{blue} 5}^2,$ writing the constant term ${\\color{purple}20}$ last:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{x^2- {\\color{red}{2}}\\cdot {\\color{blue} 5}\\cdot x + {\\color{blue} 5}^2} - {\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 -{\\color{blue} 5}^2 + {\\color{purple}20}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\left(x-5\\right)^2 - 5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2({x}^{2}-10x+20) & =-2({(x-5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+(-2)\\cdot (-5) \\\\  & =-2{(x-5)}^{2}+10\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501188 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13498b09-a219-5dfa-9293-f4041b9703a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36-(t-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500508} {:answer/id #uuid "2c4aa913-b332-54d8-be5c-49036736de34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500559} {:answer/id #uuid "78c75f6c-f643-59ba-801f-b5a91dc0bb7b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "43-(t-6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500718} {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38ba2-0edf-581d-af7c-10e7df27bc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500802} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9ac659-5538-517c-8887-28133c7a46d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a4e38ba2-0edf-581d-af7c-10e7df27bc6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "29-(t+6)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500802} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a6730d9-206d-5f68-9d5b-5aff820cffca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501073}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "82dc9974-9e37-5f33-ad85-854a2a504fcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-112299" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{t}^{2}-12t-7$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({t}^{2}+12t+7)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+12t+7\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+7\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $7$ last:\n\n$$\n{{t}^{2}+2\\cdot 6t+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+7\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(t+6)^{2}-29\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7-12t-{t}^{2} & =-((t+6)^{2}-29) \\\\  & =-(t+6)^{2}+29 \\\\  & =29-(t+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501206 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85efc12e-6686-5c83-a584-51d24265e514" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{4}-{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500745} {:answer/id #uuid "87c47d54-c33c-5791-b51c-9f273e7288ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500752} {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc4dbf-a3c1-5c4a-bb30-637dbdcc4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500795} {:answer/id #uuid "ce7d23ab-bc7a-5e58-9183-a23a3316f17d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500909} {:answer/id #uuid "ff089ad5-5112-577e-a4ad-ebbdd0861706" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10-{(x-6)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501008}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a2bc4dbf-a3c1-5c4a-bb30-637dbdcc4445" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500795} :answer-field/id #uuid "23e91312-58c2-59dc-9a23-0a2685890965" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aef8db66-e528-55a5-877b-5de5c3121fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-140270" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-3x+1)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-3x+1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}x+{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-3x+1) & =-({(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x-\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "97a0bb28-2f69-527e-9789-2acd61f99dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270603} {:answer/id #uuid "a1ddc0d9-1590-5828-933b-deda6de12aa5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270707} {:answer/id #uuid "ac58089a-07bf-5122-9205-10bdc8c984fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15-(y-5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270807} {:answer/id #uuid "b08817a0-f2a1-51b7-98ef-73e631c490f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270843} {:answer/id #uuid "c9803867-897b-5c92-9521-9f0b3409dda7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b08817a0-f2a1-51b7-98ef-73e631c490f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5-(y+5)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270843} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0bb4535-ddbc-5c9e-b8f1-ec33a353b4ed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271859}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3fe4be51-7638-5af1-be56-d66a24129d70" :question/math-academy-id "q-140321" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({y}^{2}+10y+20)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+10y+20\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+20\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${5}^{2},$ writing the constant term $20$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}+2\\cdot 5y+{5}^{2}}_{⏟}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-{5}^{2}+20\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(y+5)^{2}-5.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({y}^{2}+5y+20) & =-({(y+5)}^{2}-5) \\\\  & =-{(y+5)}^{2}+5 \\\\  & =5-(y+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00259cbe-d8e5-5392-8381-04524c3d0b22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6-{(x-2)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269259} {:answer/id #uuid "260e8dc3-9c29-57ea-b43b-c48d36a9f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} {:answer/id #uuid "59ab4b2b-b9b2-5933-a43e-16332e47f135" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270066} {:answer/id #uuid "6ccf35f0-4836-5526-b487-39bc207dbcf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270233} {:answer/id #uuid "b357bb45-c3bf-5ad2-ab72-033565c17bd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{4}-{(x+\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270869}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "260e8dc3-9c29-57ea-b43b-c48d36a9f216" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269607} :answer-field/id #uuid "66fc580c-7644-50ba-93bc-29818b02080f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271693}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "793139fd-bc0b-52b9-b28f-1f9a809139b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140332" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-x-2)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-x-2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x-2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}x+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-x-2) & =-({(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}) \\\\  & =-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{9}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{4}-{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501192 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a4d1d7e-a98a-55e8-bd06-08ce93773259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500548} {:answer/id #uuid "2c63e279-30b2-51d5-b8e5-956913e9730a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{9}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500560} {:answer/id #uuid "43ec7ab1-5c7b-5fcc-ab24-6478badcd194" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500600} {:answer/id #uuid "980a2775-c11b-5cae-b3d8-18d6a1cfca30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500778} {:answer/id #uuid "d4cb9f0b-5610-5d8c-b091-422fff9bdf3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+\\frac{3}{2})}^{2}-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a4d1d7e-a98a-55e8-bd06-08ce93773259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{(x+3)}^{2}-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500548} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ba3c8f5-6904-5eb8-80c0-90dc9cc21c69" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501075}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8898e4ea-fd1e-55a2-a1c5-4594ef5b35cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-140372" :question/problem "Rewrite $4{x}^{2}+24x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $4.$ So, we take out a factor of $4$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n4({x}^{2}+6x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+6x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-{3}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+3)}^{2}-9.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4({x}^{2}+6x) & =4({(x+3)}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-4\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =4{(x+3)}^{2}-36\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3331d0b6-f01e-59e2-bb75-e67d4ef740a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{(7x+1)}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500577} {:answer/id #uuid "3cd5cdd8-8878-58c6-9eaf-b9cafe08a766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500588} {:answer/id #uuid "4798e25d-373c-5c45-8f37-74c1becad3eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x-1)}^{2}-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500609} {:answer/id #uuid "cc3d105b-231e-5660-aea4-11b4dd11aa14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x+1)}^{2}+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500900} {:answer/id #uuid "d57e2a5d-9ff9-5289-b4aa-35eb7211d36b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x-1)}^{2}-14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500923}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3cd5cdd8-8878-58c6-9eaf-b9cafe08a766" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500588} :answer-field/id #uuid "a568522c-d69a-5ea7-b2da-992338459b41" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "07e09125-f910-5338-a71f-7baf6d758234" :question/math-academy-id "q-140376" :question/problem "Rewrite $-7{x}^{2}-14x+7$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-7.$ So, we take out a factor of $-7$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-7({x}^{2}+2x-1)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2x-1\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x-1\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $1$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-{1}^{2}-1\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+1)}^{2}-2\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-7({x}^{2}+2x-1) & =-7({(x+1)}^{2}-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+(-7)\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =-7{(x+1)}^{2}+14\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "260cd36f-3cd1-5e6e-a08e-7d3ca9370c8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500542} {:answer/id #uuid "7ba08eda-a7a0-539e-92cd-414b03d79077" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500722} {:answer/id #uuid "aea1e521-d9dc-5058-a940-b2513a2a3010" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500832} {:answer/id #uuid "b17792ba-6da9-5712-933a-ecb6ba3164b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500837} {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb62be8e-3070-5be6-b38d-3a062278e1f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500897} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6e4a4bf-3e09-5621-be81-4a8b258251af" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "fc2b2629-e323-5ab5-9be3-1b12132dec56" :question/math-academy-id "q-140379" :question/problem "Rewrite $-8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-8.$ So, we take out a factor of $-8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-8({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}x) & =-8({(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+(-8)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =-8{(x+\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501166 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cc5889c-f956-5805-828b-a07b04225e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500725} {:answer/id #uuid "805a1efe-9356-5170-a269-8ed6c903aa17" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500733} {:answer/id #uuid "d93ffed7-ac55-5ecb-a0d9-3189b1b4cd84" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500929} {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} {:answer/id #uuid "f499ebac-94ab-572a-a984-e07f21d31ab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y+\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500987}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96310d9-2183-516b-b152-b846ac135e7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500930} :answer-field/id #uuid "28822793-1aa6-5555-91d5-42b23da22347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "517afe53-217c-5ede-9811-b9bf164398f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-140380" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{y}^{2}+2y+\\frac{4}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{4}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5}y+{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{4}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({y}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}y-\\frac{4}{25}) & =-5({(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(y-\\frac{1}{5})}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272081 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d854445-4b9d-5457-8fdd-3e9aafaf84c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{45}{4}-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269970} {:answer/id #uuid "6c18b098-7360-5163-a283-eff16352c701" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270231} {:answer/id #uuid "89f2f572-b7c1-5dea-a169-978a09aab668" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2}+{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270461} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2bcef6-9c81-5bea-950e-d8b1ddb76848" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-10)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270787} {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed555ea6-dfea-51d4-876e-75d752688105" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271354} :answer-field/id #uuid "880bee47-a8c3-50a6-937c-697f411c6825" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271749}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "afc8b4da-a3f1-5590-a7d5-e7a094b395bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-140410" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+5y-5$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-5y+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-5y+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}y+{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+5y-5 & =-({(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(y-\\frac{5}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501236 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00561303-4d8b-5648-aef1-ded86fb68a13" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "38-{(x+14)}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500470} {:answer/id #uuid "15ab2670-044a-519f-a892-d7c304eec4e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500515} {:answer/id #uuid "c588653b-6c79-5422-bfe3-23e763ffffb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{4}+{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500885} {:answer/id #uuid "e7716d6f-5fc6-5f2a-ab7a-5ec082b205e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500959} {:answer/id #uuid "f65885a6-a87b-5af6-b23f-932e21b5918e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{93}{4}-{(x-\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500994}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7716d6f-5fc6-5f2a-ab7a-5ec082b205e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500959} :answer-field/id #uuid "d040eed3-515d-5c40-bf7b-ad0d3ff29d67" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501113}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eaa23e3b-bd80-5fbc-b343-c6e4d7d3776d" :question/math-academy-id "q-140420" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{x}^{2}-7x-11$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({x}^{2}+7x+11)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+7x+11\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+11\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $11$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}x+{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}+11\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}-7x-11 & =-({(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{5}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-{(x+\\frac{7}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "102a30de-999c-5721-9c7b-063f8071b140" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269413} {:answer/id #uuid "23953c7d-cf98-58a8-9973-133a833ff4f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269581} {:answer/id #uuid "e4255eb5-9f74-5ac6-9031-ca5e6cbae4ed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(2x-3)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271279} {:answer/id #uuid "e4975d6e-5d51-5f97-bc90-be1042f45ea4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(2x-3)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271284} {:answer/id #uuid "f9957bf7-7006-5741-9d61-53cef2156cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271452}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f9957bf7-7006-5741-9d61-53cef2156cf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-3)}^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271452} :answer-field/id #uuid "02699db9-8f59-5c37-91e1-d12537c34bb1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271508}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "838eecfd-7ac7-5b5a-b69c-54436c56049e" :question/math-academy-id "q-140528" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-12x+16$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-6x+8)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+8\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $8$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+8\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-6x+8) & =2((x-3)^{2}-1) \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2{(x-3)}^{2}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272128 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0245ee6f-6eed-595d-8f05-de3a8a337d94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269288} {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} {:answer/id #uuid "477fcb1e-4945-5111-8193-a1e04fd1813c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269917} {:answer/id #uuid "5bebba35-09ab-5b75-8274-fdce5263c6f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270087} {:answer/id #uuid "68b0a43b-11d0-52d9-a265-d5894d01e6ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x-\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a55a7d4-99df-5c21-8561-6b94985ce44e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269361} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c0808a8-74a4-5bb5-83bd-e46fd04336eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1e4eb76b-1e24-5200-b802-18e77326289b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140536" :question/problem "Rewrite $-5{x}^{2}-6x+\\frac{1}{5}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-5.$ So, we take out a factor of $-5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor: \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{1}{25}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}\\cdot x+{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-{(\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{25}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5({x}^{2}+\\frac{6}{5}x-\\frac{1}{25}) & =-5({(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+(-5)\\cdot (-\\frac{2}{5}) \\\\  & =-5{(x+\\frac{3}{5})}^{2}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501163 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184322 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a85fece-e40f-543c-b3c3-ae43a5175808" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient is Negative One: Odd Linear Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20969546-8cf5-58bf-9f69-aa85c2b994ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500533} {:answer/id #uuid "4346ba55-161e-55f0-af3a-5ede323b90c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{83}{4}+{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500596} {:answer/id #uuid "a7969ead-7a86-53c0-8879-105a6fba4435" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-20-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500813} {:answer/id #uuid "e1ca5c4a-5384-5160-a988-e4dc1ad1f8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{53}{4}-{(y+\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500944} {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f00d8-7485-5f2b-815c-34d67d987271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501011}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ff5f00d8-7485-5f2b-815c-34d67d987271" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501011} :answer-field/id #uuid "59a58e43-e215-570e-85ca-38eb345f6b39" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501055}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "464780e0-2b69-5233-beb8-cdc5f928ae62" :question/math-academy-id "q-142072" :question/problem "Rewrite $-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-9y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $-\\frac{1}{4}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{9}{2}y+{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-{(\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{y}^{2}+9y+\\frac{1}{4} & =-({(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{41}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{41}{2}-{(y-\\frac{9}{2})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290355 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b55aac5-2514-5dd4-ab41-73c70b5dae21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b55aac5-2514-5dd4-ab41-73c70b5dae21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288962} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f5db459-9a1a-5731-8ec2-fd3d7a0c226b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289807} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a866cbcc-d9b0-591d-97be-ca053f21a3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289147}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a866cbcc-d9b0-591d-97be-ca053f21a3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289147} :answer-field/id #uuid "94c96ca7-4a6f-5e81-9112-d6132be7b716" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290062}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a2c104a3-b59c-55e6-a052-aa1a23a45e08" :question/math-academy-id "q-218604" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}+12x+14)$ by completing the square.\n\n$-({x}^{2}+12x+14)=$\n{{field-1}}\n$-(x+$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+12x+14\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+14\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${6}^{2},$ writing the constant term $14$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}+2\\cdot 6x+{6}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-{6}^{2}+14\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x+6)^{2}-22.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}+12x+14) & =-({(x+6)}^{2}-22) \\\\  & =-(x+6)^{2}+22 \\\\  & =22-(x+6)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184212 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3202d7cc-541b-553b-8ec1-d4ea85dd0590" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Negative Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8dcca78-6127-52c8-a7fd-0263cd403f9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500963} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d83943b-fa05-5a32-815b-4e9cc7e9a241" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501041} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2625b9b2-ccd2-5138-938a-0f951e89b71a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500543} :answer-field/id #uuid "5aa7cf4a-4ced-579f-b3b1-a8420309518b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501057} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "108be57e-a123-56a8-8b6d-ef26347eebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500504} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a7e8649-8e07-5c84-b1f7-d22b5da0ebf7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501063}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "badd637f-464d-556b-9b5c-ae357a07836a" :question/math-academy-id "q-218760" :question/problem "Rewrite $-3{y}^{2}+8y-6$ by completing the square.\n\n$-3{y}^{2}+8y-6=$\n{{field-1}}\n$(y\\,-$\n{{field-2}}\n$)^{2}\\,-$\n{{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-3.$ So, we take out a factor of $-3$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+2\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2},$ writing the constant term $2$ last:\n\n$$\n{{y}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot y+{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}}_{⏟}-\\,{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-{(\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+2\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3({y}^{2}-\\frac{8}{3}y+2) & =-3({(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}+(-3)\\cdot (\\frac{2}{9}) \\\\  & =-3{(y-\\frac{4}{3})}^{2}-\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d1542e3-e27b-5f3b-a9cf-d176370aebd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "68-(z-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500497} {:answer/id #uuid "702b8582-b9b0-567b-af2b-a8dfa70c095a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500695} {:answer/id #uuid "87a9058b-6617-5b7b-a686-12d2470baa79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49-(z-7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500751} {:answer/id #uuid "9269d8f3-f3eb-5f87-9901-6dc53e2775ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "19+(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500768} {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc6cb-574c-563d-b544-da9018d44b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce1bc6cb-574c-563d-b544-da9018d44b27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30-(z+7)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500907} :answer-field/id #uuid "8320e095-8ba2-55cc-97f8-a79ff22fad04" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1ed5444-a38b-5b2e-8f5a-4a0e97b37ff2" :question/math-academy-id "q-25254" :question/problem "Rewrite $-19-14z-{z}^{2}$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $-1.$ So, we take out a factor of $-1$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n-({z}^{2}+14z+19)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+14z+19\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+19\n$$\n\nThen, we add and then subtract ${7}^{2},$ writing the constant term $19$ last:\n\n$$\n{{z}^{2}+2\\cdot 7z+{7}^{2}}_{⏟}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-{7}^{2}+19\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(z+7)^{2}-30\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-19-14z-{z}^{2} & =-((z+7)^{2}-30) \\\\  & =-(z+7)^{2}+30 \\\\  & =30-(z+7)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332891 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17a3a43a-0340-5e3c-9bcf-b1d4cc0763a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330152} {:answer/id #uuid "4b779a67-3896-55fc-be1f-97694c6c476f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330635} {:answer/id #uuid "af563452-6627-5310-af3e-8f3e9187bdf8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(x-1)}^{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331496} {:answer/id #uuid "efd2938a-788b-5b82-8a39-c673b2cf6bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332058} {:answer/id #uuid "effab392-c1df-59af-aa5f-e43acece335d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x+1)}^{2}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efd2938a-788b-5b82-8a39-c673b2cf6bd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{(x-1)}^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332058} :answer-field/id #uuid "59e8f7ca-397d-5324-b14e-634be47e4ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332374}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0793571e-bec3-5375-a73d-bbdb84782691" :question/math-academy-id "q-52522" :question/problem "Rewrite $2{x}^{2}-4x+3$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $2.$ So, we take out a factor of $2$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2})\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${1}^{2},$ writing the constant term $\\frac{3}{2}$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 1x+{1}^{2}}_{⏟}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}-{1}^{2}+\\frac{3}{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-2x+\\frac{3}{2}) & =2((x-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2{(x-1)}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332893 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29685454-9e89-546a-bdb5-50f4294edfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330317} {:answer/id #uuid "5bb811c0-7cf3-5a37-9338-fbc339b8c10b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330768} {:answer/id #uuid "70e2e8a0-02ea-5dc3-96c1-2a23a510b938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330963} {:answer/id #uuid "cd46d12a-92b5-5c2b-80b7-ef1bd3e75c53" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x-2)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331770} {:answer/id #uuid "dcaa28c1-50ea-591c-b863-be95e01f2d06" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(x+5)^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331905}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29685454-9e89-546a-bdb5-50f4294edfe5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5(x-2)^{2}+5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330317} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c22ed9b-d0d8-5857-aa56-4117fcc8f070" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332475}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5fbf174c-0450-5828-bb2d-71a05a24426d" :question/math-academy-id "q-52538" :question/problem "Rewrite $5{x}^{2}-20x+25$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $5.$ So, we take out a factor of $5$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n5({x}^{2}-4x+5)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-4x+5\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+5\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${2}^{2},$ writing the constant term $5$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 2x+{2}^{2}}_{⏟}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}-{2}^{2}+5\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-2)^{2}+1\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5({x}^{2}-4x+5) & =5((x-2)^{2}+1) \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5{(x-2)}^{2}+5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184804 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184323 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "564f2cf7-aaaf-5dea-b619-a560c3151fcc" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square on an Expression With a Positive Leading Coefficient"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d0f7330-a210-5dab-b07f-e27d8a72ece9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500496} {:answer/id #uuid "49d841e1-6a02-5efb-8678-6fe6afc6b67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500614} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae41a75-4ff5-594a-978f-7c64fa1799af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(8x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500758} {:answer/id #uuid "8bc81ce1-b560-5ed7-949e-b540fa0823c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{(8x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500760} {:answer/id #uuid "ac757b0b-83df-5393-8914-188485d81214" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500822}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d841e1-6a02-5efb-8678-6fe6afc6b67c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500614} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a5323cf-6dd2-553c-91f2-c20ecad02240" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186183433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62243cf5-4bf2-5f4d-9d71-de0fec8db876" :question/math-academy-id "q-52541" :question/problem "Rewrite $8{x}^{2}-4x$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "Notice that the leading coefficient is $8.$ So, we take out a factor of $8$ from the entire expression:\n\n$$\n8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x)\n$$\n\nLet's now complete the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}:$ \n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}x+{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}}_{⏟}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last term, we get\n\n$$\n{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8({x}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}x) & =8({(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{16}) \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-8\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\  & =8{(x-\\frac{1}{4})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186501168 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186184211 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "808c9f7b-b951-5306-bce8-efc1aec8c2b7" :knowledge-point/title "Completing the Square When the Leading Coefficient Is Negative One"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bf694ca-14ea-5e03-ae5b-c2c3fdf7de10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500494} {:answer/id #uuid "2367ac0c-5a4d-592e-a6c4-bb3ab96af7c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500539} {:answer/id #uuid "5aa52ca5-7f84-5524-9e35-b67f97468bc0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-(x+3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500652} {:answer/id #uuid "76d55117-ac24-5597-977e-26f389735915" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500713} {:answer/id #uuid "a7cd2e45-2799-51f7-8b68-c7756837046d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-(x+3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500814}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0bf694ca-14ea-5e03-ae5b-c2c3fdf7de10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-(x-3)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186500494} :answer-field/id #uuid "753b685b-ee64-5d8e-94ea-d970946416c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186501069}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5bc52b75-4c42-58c5-bcab-077cbe6faec2" :question/math-academy-id "q-80579" :question/problem "Rewrite $-({x}^{2}-6x+10)$ by completing the square." :question/worked-solution "We begin by completing the square for the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-6x+10\n$$\n\nFirst, we rewrite the coefficient of the linear term as a product with $2$ as a factor:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+10\n$$\n\nThen, we *add* and *then subtract* ${3}^{2},$ writing the constant term $10$ last:\n\n$$\n{{x}^{2}-2\\cdot 3x+{3}^{2}}_{⏟}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nThe first three terms form a perfect square, which we factor as follows:\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}-{3}^{2}+10\n$$\n\nEvaluating the last two terms, we get\n\n$$\n(x-3)^{2}+1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute this result back into our original expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-({x}^{2}-6x+10) & =-({(x-3)}^{2}+1) \\\\  & =-(x-3)^{2}-1 \\\\  & =-1-(x-3)^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
